# Claude Code — User-level instructions

## Communication style

Conversation style lives in the `daniel-voice` output style; this file holds only what has to
reach subagents, which an output style never does — everything below governs text written to
disk, not just my replies.

#### Formatting rules for written output

- No emojis unless I ask. Reference code as `file:line`.
- Paths to things I'll open (specs, plans, HTML reports, sandbox artifacts) go as short clickable markdown links, never raw: a raw path stops being clickable once it wraps. A terminal session takes `[label](file:///abs/path)`. The Claude desktop app rejects `file://`, so there use a path relative to the working directory, or the `http://127.0.0.1` URL for anything under `~/.claude/artifacts`, which the app never grants.
- Every reference to something with a URL is a markdown link: a ticket, a PR, an issue, a Slack thread, a doc page, a dashboard, an incident, a web source. Write `[PROC-1234](https://…/browse/PROC-1234)`, never a bare `PROC-1234`. Build the URL from a known pattern when the ID alone determines it. Leave the reference unlinked only when no URL can be derived, rather than guessing one.
- Match written deliverables — specs, plans, reports, any Markdown you write to disk — to what the task needs. Cover the substance without padding it out.

### Sentence-level clarity

The six rules below adapt ASD-STE100's writing rules, not its vocabulary list, and each is
checkable by rereading what you wrote:

- **One idea per sentence.** Two independent claims means two sentences. A four-line sentence carrying a fact, its evidence, and its caveat at once is the single biggest thing that makes a doc here hard to read.
- **Claim first, qualification second.** Lead with what is true, then bound it. Don't make me hold three subordinate clauses before reaching the verb.
- **Name the actor.** "kube-router enforces ingress policies", not "ingress policies are enforced". Passive voice hides who does the thing, which is usually the fact an operator needs.
- **Same name for the same thing, every time.** Pick one of rollout / deploy / apply and keep it for the whole document. Elegant variation reads as three different concepts.
- **No noun stacks past three words.** "backup target arm state check" → "the check that reads whether the backup target is armed".
- **At most one em-dash aside per paragraph.** A load-bearing fact parenthesised in an aside belongs in its own sentence.

Four more, from the Google developer documentation style guide:

- **Conditions and purpose before the instruction.** "To deploy the change, run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`", not "Run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` to deploy the change". Leading with the goal lets me skip a step that doesn't apply to me before I've read the command.
- **Nothing dates the prose.** Cut *currently*, *now*, *new*, *soon*, *latest*, *existing*, *as of this writing*. "The hook matches `auto|manual`" stays true longer than "the hook currently matches `auto|manual`". Memory files and skill docs get read months later. Use an absolute date when the timing is itself the fact.
- **Software does not perceive or want.** "The linter reports an unused import", not "the linter sees one"; "the API requires a token", not "the API wants a token". Naming the actor invites a human verb — name it and keep the verb literal.
- **Present tense, not `will`.** "The script writes the manifest, then reloads the unit." Reserve `will` for something genuinely deferred: "the file is archived the next time the backup runs."

**Say what you mean, literally.** Mannered prose substitutes metaphor and flourish for direct
statement: "a dial worth turning" for "a parameter worth varying", "this point earns its keep"
for "this point still matters". The phrase exists to display the writer, not to convey the
idea, and it is imprecise — a metaphor drags in connotations you did not choose. When a literal
phrase is available, use it.

**Compression is not clarity.** Terse means no padding, not telegraphic. Dropping the
articles and verbs out of a finding — "B2 disarmed pending cap-raise/spend-reduction
decision" — saves a few words and costs a reread; that trade is never worth it. This applies
to memory files and status lines as much as to docs.

## Config & dotfiles management (chezmoi)

My dotfiles and Claude config are managed across **two repos**. Edit the *source*, never the deployed copy under `~`; `chezmoi source-path <file>` names it, and `chezmoi-guard.sh` resyncs or warns after an edit to a deployed file.

- **Personal / general** → chezmoi, source `~/.local/share/chezmoi` (remote `DanielH2018/dotfiles`, `.chezmoiroot=home`). Owns most of `~/.claude` as **real files** under `home/private_dot_claude/`. `~/.claude/settings.json` is **generated** from `home/.chezmoitemplates/settings.base.json`. To change settings or permissions, edit that base template, then `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/settings.json`.
- **Work-specific** → `~/work-laptop-config` (plain git, remote `DanielH2018/work-laptop-config`), a symlink farm installed by `install.sh`. Owns `CLAUDE.local.md`, `docs/{integrations,enforcement}.md`, work agents, and PCI/redaction hooks — these appear as **symlinks** in `~/.claude`.

## Working in isolation

Where a task calls for isolation, isolate before editing. **Retry `EnterWorktree` once. If
it fails again, make no edits — report the failure and stop.** `repeat-failure-guard.py`
enforces the stop, and `isolation-guard.sh` backstops a background job that edits without
isolating. Reading, searching and answering happen in place, and a session inside
`.claude/worktrees/` is already isolated.

## Git conventions

- Commits are signed{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }} via the 1Password SSH agent (work laptop only){{ else }} via an OpenSSH signing key{{ end }}; `bin/check-push-signatures` rejects an unsigned push.
- The default branch is whatever `origin/HEAD` names, so read it with `git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` rather than assuming one. The dotfiles repo uses `main`; the homelab repo (`DanielH2018/server`) uses `master`. Prefer rebase over merge. Create a new commit rather than amending unless I ask for an amend.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just what changed.

## Pull requests

- **A squash merge makes the PR title the commit subject**: imperative, sentence case, naming the outcome rather than the files touched. `guard-pre-tool-use.sh` denies a title{{ if .work }} or a `planka card` title{{ end }} with a `feat:`-style prefix or a ticket id.
- A PR body exists to stop the reviewer re-deriving what you already worked out. Open on the measured symptom, name the alternatives you rejected and why, and quote real command output for every verification claim.
- Fill a repo's `.github/pull_request_template.md` when one exists rather than replacing its sections.
- The long form is the `pr-authoring` skill, which also owns diff size and commit history; `gh-stack` owns splitting.

## GitHub

`WebFetch(domain:github.com)` is denied; use `gh`. `raw.githubusercontent.com` stays fetchable for raw files `gh` doesn't cover cleanly.

## Testing

Write tests for any new code I create. Match the style and framework already used in the project.

Size them like the neighbouring test files: roughly one focused test per stated behaviour, in
the place the repo already keeps tests for that kind of change. Verify however you like while
working, but a scratch script or a quick check is not a test — don't promote it into an extra
permanent test file. This governs extras only: every behaviour the task asks for gets its test.

When a change adds or edits tests, apply the `test-scenario-hygiene` skill to the test diff
before you stop; `stop-checks.py` blocks once if you don't. Read its *Anti-patterns* section
before you write a stub or a textual assertion.

## Verification & self-checking

Don't dispatch a review subagent on your own initiative to double-check work you already finished. User instructions take precedence over skills, and this is the instruction. Asked for a review, run one. A review stage that a tool runs as a fixed step is not your initiative, so this rule does not cover it. The homelab fan-out's review phase (`fanout.py place launch` reviews every batch unless given `--no-review`) is that kind of stage: let it run, and act on its findings.

**Evidence before claims.** Say tests pass, the linter is clean or a bug is fixed only when you ran that command in this session and read its output; otherwise say the status is unverified.

## Code review

Ask review agents for **everything** they find, each finding carrying a confidence or severity score; filtering is a separate pass.

**When a reviewer says they don't understand something, fix the code — don't explain in the thread.** Clarify the code itself: rename, extract or restructure it. Leave a comment at the line that confused them only when the code cannot carry the point; a reply in the review thread helps one person once, while the code helps everyone who reads it later. Adapted from <https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/handling-comments.html>.

## Scope discipline

Finishing the whole thing has two failure modes that look finished. **Reverse states:** if you added a way in, add the way out and the way to see it — snooze needs unsnooze, close needs reopen, enable needs disable. A one-way door is a bug. **Entry points:** a behavior reachable from one place is usually also reachable from a command, a keybinding, and a settings toggle, so fixing the path you tested is not fixing the feature. When a change adds a state, a mode, or a new path through a feature, say which of the two applied; on anything smaller, just do them.

## Specs, plans & written deliverables

- When you produce a document for me to read — an implementation plan, a spec, a findings or review write-up, an audit, a handoff meant for me rather than for a successor agent — also render it as an HTML artifact for readability, in addition to the written version, not instead of it. Load the `artifact-design` skill first. The trigger is "a document I'm going to sit down and read", not the narrower "a plan": writing only the Markdown is the most common way this gets missed.
- Keep it **local by default**: write a self-contained HTML file to `~/.claude/artifacts/` and tell me the path. Publishing to claude.ai or calling the Artifact tool needs me to ask for it explicitly.
- **The link is the last thing in your reply**, after the written plan. `link-artifact.sh` emits the link; relay it verbatim.
- **In the Claude desktop app, also load that URL in the Browser pane.** The pane is the only surface that renders `.html` inside the app, and it refuses `file://`. Open the pane *and* close with the link.
- **An artifact is a rendering, not the record.** `retention-sweep` deletes an artifact after `CLAUDE_ARTIFACT_RETENTION_DAYS` (30) without an update, and artifacts are neither in git nor in the break-glass bundle. When an artifact records a decision or a plan that outlives the session, its substance goes into the repo's docs in the same PR that acts on it.
- Skip the artifact when the plan is trivial (a one-liner or single obvious step) or I've said not to.
- **Docs change in the same PR as the code they describe.** A README, a docstring or a role's `CLAUDE.md` updated in a follow-up is a doc that stays wrong until someone notices. Generated pages are the exception — their generator is the thing to change.
- **Plan in vertical slices, not horizontal layers.** Sequence a non-trivial feature as thin end-to-end slices I can exercise (curl, browser, a real test), middle-out: API contract and mock data, UI against the mock, service layer, DB migrations, business logic, error handling. Stack-order planning leaves nothing runnable until the end; slices let me resteer at 100-200 lines.

## Tool use & context discipline

A long session runs out of context from many medium tool calls, not from one big one. Every extra turn also re-spends a turn's worth of reasoning, so the cheapest saving is a turn I never take. Prefer the one command that answers the question over three that circle it.

- `jsonq` is installed and allowlisted for JSON work that shell tools can't express, where `python3 -c` costs a prompt. It has no attribute access; `jsonq --functions` lists what it can call.
- `find … -exec` is denied, because `-exec` runs any command. To search the files `find` selects, use `rg`, the Grep tool, or `find … -print0 | xargs -0 grep -l <pattern>`.
- To ask claude-guard how it judges a command, write the commands as JSONL `{"command": "…"}` records with the Write tool and run `claude-guard replay <file> --deny`. A probe that names a test command inline is judged as that command.
- To run the D5 takeover check on daniel-box, run `takeover-check --label <pr> --render <dir> --src <dir> --tags <a,b>` instead of the bare sudo line. It takes the same flags, accepts relative paths, merges stderr into stdout, and can be piped or redirected. The grant is the operator's account; as another user it fails on sudo's password prompt.
- To learn a fact about a secret file without reading it, run `secretq`: `keys FILE` lists a SOPS file's key names, `has FILE KEY` exits 0 or 1, `encrypted FILE` checks every value is ciphertext, `sha256 FILE` hashes a SOPS file, `kubeconfig [FILE]` shows contexts and servers, and `ssh-config [HOST]` shows host lines. `--rev REV` reads a file as git holds it at REV. It never prints a value, and `secretq --help` has the rest.
- To read a systemd unit, run `unitq cat [--user] <unit>` or `unitq show [--user] <unit> <Property>…` instead of `systemctl cat` or an Exec/Environment `systemctl show`. It keeps plain values such as numbers, durations and paths, the Exec binary and flag names, and replaces the rest with placeholders.
- To read root-only host state on daniel-box or daniel-server, run `rootq` instead of sudo. Tier 1 (`stat`, `sha256`, `matches PATH FILE`) prints no file content. Tier 2 (`nft`, `ufw`, `sshd`, `crontab`, `cat PATH`) prints redacted output from fixed sources. `rootq --help` names the path allowlist. The grant is the operator's account. Don't probe sudo capability with `sudo -n true`; the guard refuses it.
- To stop a process this session started, such as a stray pytest, run `killown [--dry-run] PATTERN` instead of `pkill -f`. It matches PATTERN against command lines but signals only this session's own processes, detached ones included. It works only inside a Claude Code session, so not over ssh.
- To read the environment, run `envq NAME…` for values or `envq --names [REGEX]` for names instead of `env | grep`. It prints `set` in place of a credential.
- To read GitHub, run `ghread` instead of `gh`: PRs (view, list, checks, diff, comments), issues, runs and their logs, an `api` GET with `--paginate`/`--jq`, a `graphql -f query='…'` read (a document containing `mutation` is refused), and `stack view`. It is allowlisted and never writes. Use `--out "$TMPDIR/x"` instead of a `>` redirect. It needs the sandbox off, like `gh`, because sandboxed gh cannot reach its keychain token.
- To re-run the failed jobs of a CI run in a repo you own, run `gh-rerun-failed <run-id> [-R owner/repo]` instead of `gh run rerun --failed`. It refuses another owner's repo, a run that has not finished with a failure, and a run on its third attempt. It is allowlisted and needs the sandbox off, like `gh`.
- To read Docker, run `dockq` instead of `docker`: `ps`, `images`, `info`, `logs --tail N`, `manifest inspect`, `compose ps`/`logs`, and an `inspect` that prints fixed fields and never the environment. To look up an image's tags or digest, run `dockq tags <repo>` or `dockq digest <ref>` instead of a hand-built registry curl; both cover ghcr.io, Docker Hub and a loopback mirror via `--registry localhost:5002`. It is allowlisted, and it needs the sandbox off to reach the Docker socket.
- To change Docker state, run `dockrun` instead of `docker`: `run` (always `--rm`), `pull`, `rm`/`exec`/`logs` on containers it started, and `compose up`/`down`/`ps`/`logs`/`exec` for the cwd repo's compose files. It refuses privileged and host-namespace flags, the Docker socket, bind mounts outside the repo or `$TMPDIR`, and a writable bind that reaches `.git` or `.claude` (mount those `:ro`). It is allowlisted, while bare `docker` writes prompt, and it needs the sandbox off to reach the Docker socket.
- To wait on anything (a landing, CI, a file, a process, a systemd unit, a k8s rollout), run `cc-wait <source>`; `cc-wait --list` names the sources. `claude-guard` runs the call in the background where a task notification can wake you and in the foreground where one cannot, and it denies a hand-written `sleep`, `tail -f` or `--watch` wait. To bound a wait, pass `--budget S`, which every source takes. On exit 75 the budget elapsed and the wait is still open: re-run the command it prints.

## Delegating to subagents

Delegate a search that would take 3+ exploratory calls, and keep only the findings. Load the `orchestrating-subagents` skill before fanning out subagents or building a Workflow.

## Compaction policy

`pre-compact.sh` re-injects branch and push state, files modified, test commands run, and the "preserve corrections and error strings verbatim" reminder, so don't restate them. Carry across what the hook *cannot* derive: test **pass/fail results**, architectural decisions, and next steps. When space forces cuts, drop in ascending priority (cut the first survivors last): completed work → active work → errors encountered → user corrections. A user correction is the last thing to go.

Don't reach for `/compact` yourself. Auto-compaction fires at about 600k tokens.

